"""The Excel export saved as `.md`. Two faults, stacked.

**One.** `filenameFromDisposition` fell back to a hard-coded
`benchmark-<id>.md`. That was honest while Markdown was the only export
and the only caller was the benchmark flow; it became a lie the moment a
second format existed.

**Two, and the reason the fallback ran at all:** the browser will not
hand `Content-Disposition` to JavaScript across origins unless the
server says it may. The web app is on :3000 and the API on :8000, so
every download has been taking the fallback — which means the Markdown
export has been saving as `benchmark-<id>.md` rather than the
`decision-<id>.md` the server chose, and nobody noticed because the
extension happened to be right.

So: expose the header, and make the fallback carry an extension its
caller chose rather than one this function assumes.
"""

import pathlib

ROOT = pathlib.Path("e:/VinAI/RoboMind_project/P-011")

# --- 1. let the browser read the name the server picked --------------------
main = ROOT / "apps/api/planbench_api/main.py"
text = main.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
old = """        allow_methods=["*"],
        allow_headers=["*"],
    )"""
new = """        allow_methods=["*"],
        allow_headers=["*"],
        # Without this the browser hides `Content-Disposition` from
        # JavaScript on any cross-origin read — and the web app is on a
        # different port from the API in every local setup. The download
        # helper then cannot see the filename the server chose and falls
        # back to one it guessed, which is how the Excel export came to
        # save itself as `.md`.
        expose_headers=["Content-Disposition"],
    )"""
assert old in text
text = text.replace(old, new, 1)
main.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")

# --- 2. a fallback that cannot invent an extension -------------------------
charts = ROOT / "apps/web/src/lib/charts.ts"
